{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## RCA — proven root cause\n\n**The mechanism.** `test_bridge_preview_under_sync_contention_heldout.py:77` bounds the\n`rebar bridge preview` subprocess at `timeout=15`, overriding the harness default of 60\n(`sync_contention_harness.py:36`). That bound is not arbitrary — it is the LOCK-FREEDOM ORACLE.\nA background thread holds the tracker write lock and only releases it after\n`release.wait(timeout=60)`, so a reader that BLOCKED on the lock would take ~60s. The bound\ndiscriminates: fast = lock-free, ~60s = blocked.\n\nThe defect is that **15s sits far too close to the fast path to separate the two failure modes**.\nMeasured on this machine (load 9.88, i.e. already contended):\n\n    preview WITH the writer locks held : 1.427  1.129  1.189  1.203  1.239  s\n    preview WITHOUT contention         : 1.134  1.097  1.149                s\n\nContention costs ~0.1s. The bound therefore allows only ~12x headroom over the fast path, while\nthe signature it must distinguish from is ~50x away. On a constrained runner, AMBIENT SLOWNESS\ncrosses 15s long before it approaches the blocking signature — so the oracle reports 'blocked'\nwhen the machine was merely slow. The changeable artifact is the literal `timeout=15`.\n\n**Rival hypotheses, and how they were killed.**\n- *H2: preview genuinely blocks on the writer lock sometimes (a real lock-freedom regression, and\n  the test is correctly catching it).* **REJECTED** — the with-contention runs complete in ~1.2s\n  while the lock is provably held (the test asserts the pre-acquire succeeded and that the pass\n  lock is unmodified afterwards). A blocked reader could not finish in 1.2s; it would wait out\n  the holder's 60s. This was the hypothesis that mattered, because if it had held, the correct\n  action would have been to fix `bridge preview`, not the test.\n- *H3: macOS is structurally slower on this path.* Not the mechanism. macOS CI being slower is\n  the TRIGGER; the defect is a bound that cannot tolerate it.\n\n**Legitimacy: `defect`.** Cited authority: AGENTS.md — 'A flaky test is a BUG to root-cause,\nnever a retry… Retrying until green slows development, wastes tokens, and erodes CI as a\nregression oracle.' A test that fails on ambient slowness erodes exactly that.\n\n**Attribution: `424f-bc35-b7ca-419a`, commit `43e822a83fc8` ('Make bridge preview lock-free and\nauditable', 2026-08-08).** That commit introduced both the lock-free preview and this oracle,\nincluding the `timeout=15` literal — verified by `git show 43e822a83fc8` on the test path, which\nshows the line added there. The date test passes cleanly: the implicated line was introduced by\nthat commit, not inherited, so this is not an unmet-obligation case and blame is pointing at the\nright change. Class: `plan_defect` — the originating work shipped a bound that works on a\ndeveloper laptop and cannot survive a constrained CI runner.\n\n**Caveat on my own earlier measurement.** My first with-change timing read 54.54s and I reported\na regression as 'decisive' on that single run. It was cold-start plus four concurrent subagents\nloading this box; every subsequent run was ~1.2s for the subprocess and ~4s for the test. One\ntiming sample on a loaded machine is not evidence."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787890954005480001,"uuid":"46b647eb-783f-4330-a17e-04ff303fc988"}